WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:09.468
This clip shows the WaPOR twenty metre downscaler, over the Chokwe irrigation scheme in the lower Limpopo.

00:00:10.294 --> 00:00:14.013
Open the data catalogue with Explore data.

00:00:14.671 --> 00:00:19.860
The analysis tools sit under Water Use Monitoring and Analysis.

00:00:20.523 --> 00:00:32.751
Open the twenty metre downscaler. It sharpens WaPOR evapotranspiration from three hundred metres to twenty, so individual fields become readable.

00:00:33.409 --> 00:00:41.922
Choose the dekad, the ten day period you want. Every dekad WaPOR publishes is listed.

00:00:42.591 --> 00:00:51.014
Then the model. The ensemble is the most accurate; we use CatBoost here because it is the fastest.

00:00:51.683 --> 00:01:04.610
Click Polygon, then click each corner of your area. Click the first corner again to close the shape, and press Done. Keep it under twenty square kilometres.

00:01:10.727 --> 00:01:18.660
Run the analysis. The work happens on the server, so the map stays usable while it runs.

00:01:19.329 --> 00:01:30.452
The twenty metre field lands on the map, clipped to the area you drew. Irrigated blocks that three hundred metre pixels blur together now stand apart.

00:01:31.113 --> 00:01:39.691
Terria draws your input polygon over the result. Switch the result off and the twenty metre field reads clearly.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:52.627
The legend reads in millimetres per dekad. The native three hundred metre layer sits underneath on the same colour scale, so switching the top layer off compares the two directly.

00:01:53.287 --> 00:02:02.960
The panel reports bias, error and correlation against that coarse input, and links the twenty metre GeoTIFF for download.

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:11.637
That is the downscaler. The time-series tool beside it runs the same area across a whole season.
